From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 99992: trust the Device Information TLV, not the frame source

A CMDU that crossed a wireless backhaul cannot be attributed by its Ethernet
source address. Measured on hardware 2026-08-03 with the mesh chained as
controller <- 8g <- x8:

  tcpdump on the controller, every 1905 frame that arrived:
    19x 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b     (8g bSTA)
    10x 86:f7:56:41:01:d3     (own, outgoing)

x8 never appeared under any address of its own. Everything originating beyond
8g arrives wearing 8g. Worse, ieee1905 then holds two links for that single
address:

  3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b -> ca:8e:1d:ca:bc:ae  direct=true    (8g)
  3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b -> e6:a9:d2:c1:98:3f  direct=false   (x8)

i1905_link_lookup_partial() in cmdu_input.c takes whichever comes first, so the
origin of a two-hop CMDU is decided by list order.

CMDU_SA_IS_ALMAC does not rescue this. Three Topology Queries were sent from 8g
with the source deliberately set to its AL MAC; all three arrived at the
controller carrying 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b. The uplink rewrites it.

The measured consequence was a silent swap in the database - x8 uplink recorded
against node 8g, and node x8 left holding a value that had been correct that
morning - while both agents reported correctly over ubus the whole time. Node
depth, apmld, affiliated_ap and metrics for the far node all follow from this.

A Topology Response carries a Device Information TLV, which is the sender
stating its own AL MAC and is mandatory in that message. Prefer it. When it
disagrees with the frame, the frame is the relay and the TLV is the author.

This does not fix messages that carry no such TLV; those still need the origin
resolved another way.
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c
@@ -425,6 +425,41 @@
 		return -1;
 	}
 
+	/* The Ethernet source address cannot identify the sender across a
+	 * wireless backhaul. Measured on hardware 2026-08-03, mesh chained as
+	 * controller <- 8g <- x8: every CMDU originating beyond 8g arrived with
+	 * source 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b, which is 8g bSTA, and ieee1905 held two links
+	 * for that one address - one to 8g (direct) and one to x8 - so
+	 * i1905_link_lookup_partial() resolved the origin to whichever entry came
+	 * first. Three Topology Queries sent with the source deliberately set to
+	 * an AL MAC all arrived carrying 8g bSTA instead, so CMDU_SA_IS_ALMAC does
+	 * not survive the uplink either.
+	 *
+	 * The visible result was x8 uplink recorded on node 8g and the morning
+	 * value left standing on node x8, while both agents reported correctly
+	 * over ubus. Nothing in the log said so, because from this side the
+	 * attribution succeeded.
+	 *
+	 * The Device Information TLV is the sender describing itself and is
+	 * mandatory in a Topology Response, so prefer it over the frame.
+	 */
+	if (tv[TOPOLOGY_RESPONSE_DEVICE_INFORMATION_TYPE_IDX][0]) {
+		const struct tlv_device_info *di = (struct tlv_device_info *)
+			tv[TOPOLOGY_RESPONSE_DEVICE_INFORMATION_TYPE_IDX][0]->data;
+
+		if (!hwaddr_is_zero((uint8_t *)di->aladdr) &&
+		    memcmp(di->aladdr, n->almacaddr, 6)) {
+			struct node *real = cntlr_find_node(c, (uint8_t *)di->aladdr);
+
+			if (real) {
+				dbg("%s: origin " MACFMT " is a relay; TLV says "
+				    MACFMT ", using that\n", __func__,
+				    MAC2STR(n->almacaddr), MAC2STR(di->aladdr));
+				n = real;
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	cntlr_mark_agent_if_supported(c, n->almacaddr,
 				      tv[TOPOLOGY_RESPONSE_SUPPORTED_SERVICE_IDX][0]);
 
